.SH Request/response channels
Channels 0\-2 (CONTROL, COMMAND, STATE) use a strict request/response
pattern.
The client sends a request and waits for exactly one response (or, in
the case of COMMAND, a sequence of stdout/stderr/ complete messages).
.PP
The client bindings implement this with:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Python:\f[R] \f[CR]await self._recv(channel)\f[R] which awaits an
\f[CR]asyncio.Queue.get()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]TypeScript:\f[R] A Promise added to a per\-channel queue; resolved
by the reader loop when the response arrives.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]C:\f[R] Synchronous read loop that blocks until the matching
response arrives on the target channel.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Java:\f[R] \f[CR]queue.poll(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)\f[R] on a
per\-channel \f[CR]BlockingQueue\f[R].
.SH Server\-push channels
Channels 3\-5 (OBSERVE, DEBUG, PTY) support server\-initiated messages
in addition to request/response.
These push messages arrive asynchronously and are dispatched to
registered callbacks.
.PP
