.SH Channel availability
All channels are available after successful authentication.
CHAN_CONTROL is the only channel that accepts messages before
authentication \(en specifically, the \f[CR]auth\f[R] message.
.PP
Sending a message on any other channel before authentication results in
a \f[CR]ServerError\f[R] / \f[CR]BC_ERR_SERVER\f[R] response.
.SH Ordering guarantees
\f[B]Within a channel:\f[R] Messages are processed strictly in order.
A response to request N always arrives before the response to request
N+1 on the same channel.
.PP
\f[B]Between channels:\f[R] Messages on different channels may be
interleaved.
The server processes requests from all channels concurrently.
.PP
\f[B]Server\-push ordering:\f[R] Push events (OBSERVE, DEBUG, PTY)
include sequence numbers (\f[CR]seq\f[R] in observe events) that allow
clients to reconstruct the original order relative to command responses.
.PP
\f[B]Practical implication:\f[R] If you send an \f[CR]eval\f[R] on
CHAN_COMMAND and a \f[CR]get_var\f[R] on CHAN_STATE simultaneously, the
responses may arrive in either order.
The client bindings handle this correctly because each channel has its
own response queue.
